table_validation_us <- tar_read(overall_validation_us, store="../../_targets") %>%
  xtable(digits=c(0, rep(1, 3), rep(2, 4)))
print(table_validation_us, booktabs=FALSE, floating=FALSE)
@
\end{center}
\end{table}

\section{Testing phase} \label{testing_results}
The seven models from the best subset were joined by the two Forecast Hub comparison models, the COVIDhub-baseline and the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble, for the testing phase analysis. Generally, we found that the original THieF models with a fourth root transformation and the COVIDHub-4\_week\_ensemble showed the best performance out of the nine total models across all of the smaller evaluations. The THieF ensembles displayed more middling performance, though they almost always beat the COVIDhub-baseline.

We illustrated the forecasts made during the testing phase through plotting 1- to 28-day ahead incident hospitalization point, 50\% interval, and 95\% interval forecasts for the U.S. national location Figure \ref{fig:figure_forecasts_plot1} from four models of interest: the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble, the COVIDhub-baseline, a poor performing model (sarima\_s7- noTransform), and a top performing model (THieF\_6wk-4root). From this figure, the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble appears to have best followed the true confirmed hospitalizations compared to the other three models, especially during the Omicron wave's period most rapid increase. Note also that for most forecast weeks the COVIDhub-baseline had the widest 95\% interval forecasts of any of the displayed models (recall that narrower intervals tend to be more desirable, as long as they capture the true value). The results shown by Figure \ref{fig:figure_forecasts_plot1} were mirrored (and perhaps further explained) by the following stratified evaluations.

\begin{figure}%[h]
\caption{U.S. national incident hospitalization forecasts during the testing phase from select models}
\label{fig:figure_forecasts_plot1}
<<forecast_models_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, height=12, width=10>>=
tar_read(plot_models_1, store="../../_targets") + theme_bw()
@
\end{figure}

